[Music]  [Applause]  why would I ever hire you  Oh over one of the finance majors  sitting outside of my office I took a  deep breath my heart rate soaring and I  blurted out because you can train any  monkey in a soup to run a financial  model I never forgot that interview  question but I do wish that I had a  second chance to answer it and let me  tell you I would answer it  very differently today I want to talk to  you about why hiring musicians and  artists can strengthen businesses and  teams my focus will be on music given  the influence its had on my life and my  hope is the next time you see a  non-traditional resume instead of  putting it in the reject pile you'll  move it to the top of your consideration  set music has been a part of my life  before I was even born my parents named  me after hearing pianist Eliseo della  Rocha perform and I was an adamant a  covenant my Kincaid isolate age of 8 I  picked up a tiny toy size violin and I  squished squeaked out my first attempt  of twinkle twinkle little did I know to  see hours of isolated practicing the  heated arguments with my brother over  how to play a phrase of Mendelssohn or  my realized dream of performing under  the bright lights of Carnegie Hall would  actually prepare me for careers in  business  your abstract and orchestra carries a  striking resemblance to a business you  have strings winds and the brass  consider these similar to sales finance  and HR each specialty has its own skill  set personality and unique function yet  all of them all just as the divisions of  a company unique skill set yet all the  instruments need to work seamlessly  together in order to perform a dazzling  Brahms Symphony just as the divisions of  a company need to collaborate seamlessly  in order to run a business an orchestra  when the time comes the cacophony of  last-minute tuning and practicing  subsides the conductor raises his arms  and the musicians take the stage to  unveil some of the most powerful  representations of teamwork and high  performance and it's in this Orchestra  where I learned some really valuable  lessons that I still turn to today so  let's look at five common interview  questions and how a musician might  answer them  question one tell me about a time you've  managed conflict I'm a violinist and  violinists are the Divas of the  orchestra  we're opinionated we're competitive and  we all want to play the solos so you can  imagine the first time I was tasked to  lead an all violin practice session  where we had to agree on how we wanted  to play our part there were more  opinions in that room than there were  people so how do I get this group of  prima donnas to agree I suggested that  we demonstrate our ideas through playing  and pretty quickly we found that certain  phrasing and styles were better than  others and we were able to agree on a  vision that we were eager to execute and  bring back to our orchestral team  question two how do you build  credibility on a team my friend Sam is  an accomplished organist who won a  scholarship to a prestigious university  to pursue conducting and later moved on  to a career in business in government  life takes Sam far from the organ and  podium today  but he fondly recalls learning about  accountability and its importance went  on a team through his musical training  he asked me to imagine walking into a  room with a million items on my to-do  list but the manager needs me in that  room the meeting starts and it's  immediately clear there's no agenda no  goals no paths for discussion how many  of you have been in a meeting like this  they're frustrating they're discouraging  and they make us lose faith in the  person who called it Sam likens the  familiar scenario to his work as a  conductor his ability to lead and  influence a choir hinged on  demonstrating his respect for their time  through preparation and ownership of his  artistic responsibilities now as a  manager Sam is able to more easily  establish accountability on teams and  acknowledge when he's losing trust among  them and rectify that situation quickly  question three tell me about a skill  you've had to learn over time musicians  have resilience diligence and grit the  orchestra isn't just about the conductor  or the concert master just as a business  isn't only about senior level management  each individual in the orchestra has  spent years developing their craft in  order to play their assigned part Joel  Peterson the chairman of JetBlue Airways  and a professor here at the Graduate  School of Business had all of his  children get involved in music because  in his words there's no way to cram all  the smart kids in school will always  inevitably find a shortcut or a way  around the system but he believes you  can't do that with music music requires  putting in the time the hard work and  the discipline in order to improve your  performance and this ability to develop  resilience a work ethic and a sense of  one's self becomes increasingly  important as a career growth question  for what would you do in a situation  where you have multiple tasks to  complete but no conceivable way of  finishing them all by the deadline  musicians frequently become great  practitioners of the 80  a principle that ability to identify  where the most value might come from I  prioritize certain actions over others  Bess a former consultant and now a  founder of her own successful venture  told me that she hated practicing the  violin the hours spent alone in a room  knowing that she was not spending time  with friends or other fun activities  made her resent practicing so what was  the solution quickly identify the most  important or difficult passages and  focus on mastering those first Elvis  didn't mean that putting in hours didn't  matter but she was able to learn how to  use her time more effectively now by the  time that she got her first job  Bess was able to identify the more  valuable parts of a project faster than  her peers and she was a better  contributor and teammate because of it  question five how would your boss or  your coworkers describe you from an  early age musician's experience coaching  at a forensic level I vividly remember  peering over my violin teachers shoulder  to better understand how to hold my bow  or play a finger twisting passage of  Bach this ability to be coached is  something we aspire to as business  leaders we seek feedback and we hope to  monitor ourselves when there's no coach  around us with the musicians receiving  feedback doesn't need to be taught  because the concept is already ingrained  just as a conductor might make a minor  adjustment to better tease out a melody  musicians are ready to hear from their  managers or mentors on how to present  more effectively tweaks a financial  model or more meaningfully interact with  their peers so when we return to the  orchestra as a whole it's a really  powerful training ground for teamwork  influence and leadership all at the same  time even with the conductor at the helm  the whole is undoubtedly greater than  the sum of the parts a successful  performance depends on knowing who's  playing what role around you and being  able to anticipate the actions of others  to quote dr. Condoleezza Rice the  former Secretary of State professor at  Stanford and a highly accomplished  classical pianist musicians need to let  their creativity humanity and  vulnerability come through despite all  the hours invested in their craft  anything can happen when you're onstage  and you have to push through and learn  from your mistakes  even today I know I found the ability to  stand in front of you because of years  of experience performing under bright  lights and in front of huge crowds so if  I could rewind that clock I walk once  again down the hall of that investment  bank I would stand a little taller as I  walk past the other typical interviewees  I would feel more comfortable in my  first suit I would know why my resume  was at the top of the pile why should  you hire me over the others in the hall  because I manage conflict  I value accountability I work tirelessly  at my craft I'm coachable but most  importantly because I am a musician  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

